Keep DirectDraw vertex buffers in CPU memory for WebGL.

Links 2003 unlocks a vertex buffer, draws it, and then updates diffuse colors
through the retained lock pointer before a second draw. The WebGL upload path
evicts the unlocked CPU shadow after the first draw, leaving that pointer
unmapped (LinksMMIII.exe + 0x66c4c).

Use DirectDraw's existing system-memory vertex-buffer path for WebGL, as is
already done for pre-D3D7 buffers. Each draw then reads the current CPU data
through the existing upload path. Preserve the application's reported caps and
leave desktop Wine's buffer selection unchanged.

diff --git a/dlls/ddraw/vertexbuffer.c b/dlls/ddraw/vertexbuffer.c
--- a/dlls/ddraw/vertexbuffer.c
+++ b/dlls/ddraw/vertexbuffer.c
@@ -477,5 +477,9 @@ HRESULT d3d_vertex_buffer_create(struct d3d_vertex_buffer **vertex_buf,
      *
      * This improves performance in Prince of Persia 3D. */
-    buffer->sysmem = ((buffer->Caps & D3DVBCAPS_SYSTEMMEMORY) || buffer->version < 7);
+    /* WebGL uploads evict unlocked GPU buffers' CPU shadows. Legacy D3D7
+     * applications also update retained lock pointers between draws; use the
+     * CPU path so those pointers remain valid and each draw sees their writes. */
+    buffer->sysmem = ((buffer->Caps & D3DVBCAPS_SYSTEMMEMORY) || buffer->version < 7
+            || ddraw_webgl_enabled());

     wined3d_mutex_lock();
